            "text": {
                "initialization": "",
                "initializationLanguageId": "",
                "source": "",
                "sourceLanguageId": "kotlin",
                "specification": "# cpx\n\n## Architectural role\n\nThe root is the callable compiler function graph for the C+ compiler architecture in SPEC.TECH.md. It composes phase functions, processing functions, object-instance prototypes, library suites, and named link arguments.\n\n## Function model\n\n- Composites and processing nodes are functions, not prototypes. Their links are materialized as explicit function arguments.\n- Each processing node pops packets from input arguments, invokes one coherent operation from a library suite, and pushes results to outgoing arguments.\n- Libraries box free-standing function suites or method suites associated with declared object-instance prototypes. They contain the bulk algorithms.\n- Types are object-instance prototypes and define packet shapes; they do not perform computation.\n- Link names are lowerCamelCase identifiers and are unique for every incident node. Numeric suffixes are used when a function has repeated argument channels.\n\n## Layout model\n\nThe graph is a relaxed left-to-right fiber: generators precede connected siblings, later stages progress rightward, and sinks settle at the far right. Fibers may be jagged vertically to route links and keep port labels readable. Backward dependencies are retained as dashed links and minimized.\n\n## Pipeline\n\n`readSourceFiles` -> `parseSource` -> `buildAst` -> `indexDeclarations` -> `resolveModulesAndCImports` -> `resolveSymbolsAndScopes` -> `resolveTypes` -> `expandStructuralCpx` -> `stabilizeTypeUniverse` -> `expandReflectiveCpx` -> `loadOrCreateGenericSpecialization` -> `lowerCplus` -> `buildCSubsetAstAndDependencies` -> `emitCAndSourceMap`.\n\nThe `runCompilation` function coordinates phase ordering and cancellation. `error_diagnosticsAndIncrementalWorkspace` and `serveLspAndCli` consume results through explicit packet arguments.\n\n## Architectural invariants\n\n1. Source origins remain stable through parsing, expansion, rewriting, lowering, and emission.\n2. SemanticModel is authoritative for compiler and IDE behavior.\n3. Structural CPX reaches a fixed point before reflective CPX.\n4. Reflection begins only after type-universe stabilization.\n5. Lowering retains origin metadata.\n6. Generated C symbols are deterministic and collision-free.\n7. Diagnostics and source maps remain structured and routable.\n8. Incremental compilation follows dependency links and invalidates affected specializations.\n\n## Identifier conventions\n\n- Libraries MUST use `lib_`, `library_`, `_lib`, or `_library`; the current library nodes use the `library_` prefix.\n- Error handlers MUST use `err_`, `error_`, `_err`, or `_error`; the current error handler uses the `error_` prefix.\n- Library argument links remain camelCase function arguments and are not library identifiers.\n\n## Naming semantics\n\n- Processing nodes and composites are callable functions and therefore use imperative verb identifiers.\n- Links are noun-named channels. They carry information packets, object-instance prototype values, or library service capabilities; repeated channel bases use numeric suffixes.\n- Types remain object-instance prototypes, while libraries remain named suites of functions or methods.\n\n- Object and structure prototype identifiers use PascalCase, for example `SemanticModel`, `CSubsetAst`, and `SourceMap`.",
                "specificationLanguageId": "markdown",
                "tests": "{\"cases\":[]}",
                "testsLanguageId": "json",
                "aiInstructions": "",
                "aiInstructionsLanguageId": "markdown"
            },
